        "changelog": "Root-caused the recurring permission problem behind the last several releases, found live while debugging 1.1.0.3's \"stat: Permission denied\" noise: UnixPermissions.MatchTo, when healing a folder using a plain media FILE as its reference (the common case - a movie's own folder healed against the movie file itself), copied the reference's mode bits onto the folder verbatim. A regular file naturally has no execute bits at all, so this silently stripped the folder's own execute/traverse bit on every run - confirmed live against a real folder left at \"drw-rw-r--\" (no execute for anyone, including its own owner) after being \"healed\". This has been present since permission healing was first added (0.8.28.0) and runs unconditionally on every run for a movie's theme-song folder once \"Fetch theme songs\" is on - very likely the actual reason permissions kept needing to be fixed by hand between runs throughout this project's history, since the healing meant to fix them was the one silently breaking them again. MatchTo now derives a folder's execute bits from its own read/write bits (the same \"chmod +X\" convention every Unix tool uses), regardless of what the reference file's own bits look like.",
